# Changelog

This is a living atlas. This file records reader-visible additions and corrections, not every commit or training run. An editorial
change does not imply a new experiment. The dated run and audit records in each entry remain authoritative for scientific scope.
Unreleased changes are not a tagged release or a DOI deposit.

## 1.1.0 (2026-11-14)

### Added

- METHODS adds Section 10, a rank-coordinate randomised quasi-Monte Carlo estimator of a network's accuracy under a known input
  law, with its construction, unbiasedness argument, full cost accounting and preregistered evaluation. On 48 noisy copies of ARC's
  public AlgZoo second-argmax example, its truth-corrected MSE at the primary budget was .380026 of equal-cost plain Monte Carlo
  (95% CI .338419–.430555). It is a hybrid estimator, slower in wall time, tested on copies of one network only; the controls
  (RI showed no gain, RAW also beat Monte Carlo) and the untested causal interpretation are retained. Scientific run 2026-11-14.
- `code/rank_rqmc.py` and `code/test_rank_rqmc.py` (MIT) implement the four estimators, download the public example network, test the
  transform and recompute any per-scale row of Table 10.4. REFERENCES adds twelve records for the section.

### Changed

- AlgZoo second-argmax and median counts follow a 2026-11-14 specificity audit, described in METHODS Section 9.1: an earlier proof
  stays proven only if its circuit keeps at least .01 more than the strongest of 64 random circuits matched by output effect on each
  of ten fresh 4,096-row sets, with three donors and every original gate still passing. Second-argmax moves from the iteration-12
  snapshot (132 proven / 33 read / 115 unexplained) to analysis iteration 61 with the audit applied: 16 proven / 257 read /
  7 unexplained. The 16 are four width-2 whole-network exceptions, two resample proofs and ten flagged optimal-only tables, so six
  count without the optimal-only exception. The entry adds the iteration 13–61 mechanism families (scalar fans, nonfinal sections,
  discrete identity quotients, width-3 carriers and helper floors) as READ readings grouped by mechanism, with each group's audit
  outcome. Median moves from 100 / 0 / 0 to 18 / 82 / 0: all 18 are width-2 whole-network exceptions, and its two width-4
  empty-complement forms become READ. Every earlier limitation and failed test is retained.
- README and INDEX date the external-zoo snapshots by analysis iteration only, without naming an analysis agent.
- Every entry now shows its circuit picture right after the opening story, before the fact table; long identifiers and equations wrap on phones.

## 1.0.0 (2026-11-14)

First tagged release, archived on Zenodo. Networks: Hugging Face `jc1122/circuit-atlas-nets` revision `a2754d3`;
snapshots: `jc1122/circuit-atlas-snapshots` revision `3fcddff`. Rendered site: GitHub Pages.

### Changed

- README's figures now show the active count indicators and a worked division step, with values on the information paths and
  frozen/trained labels. Explicit image widths and shorter drawing proportions keep the main text readable on phones. The division
  picture contrasts parallel internal comparisons with a written-state feedback loop; its counts remain hand-built design sizes.
  Partial mechanism status, omitted wiring and all existing evidence tables and limitations are retained.
- README now opens with a letter-counting picture, four linked finding stories and a three-entry tour. Detailed scrub failures,
  attribution ranges, acceptance rules and historical counts remain below the catalogue, with their original qualifications.
- The scratchpad size figure now shows how written intermediate state lets a hand-built compare-and-subtract circuit be reused.
  The original division, square-root and multiplication counts and their different units remain in a linked text table; the drawing
  does not claim that trained networks are this small. The counting picture marks supplied and trained parts, with partial status.
- INDEX adds topic jumps in plain words; GLOSSARY starts with networks, weights, circuits and training before the technical terms.
  SITE proposes a static reading site for owner review only. No hosting, build workflow or publication setting is enabled.

- The shared catalogue covers the current entries and distinguishes seeds, best/final snapshots, screening cells and sampled
  external-zoo certificates. Task performance and mechanism acceptance are separate claims.
- Reader routes, reuse instructions, archive-coverage caveats and licence/attribution scope are explicit in README and INDEX.
- STYLE and TEMPLATE require model/data documentation, bounded negative-result reporting, accessible figure equivalents and
  a distinction between retraining, archive rescoring and file-integrity checks.
- Citation metadata describes both transformer studies and external recurrent-model snapshots. Cite the actual entry and revisions
  used; no new version number, release date or DOI is assigned here.
- REFERENCES reconciles inherited task citations with source types, identifier anchors and entry links. Metadata checks are
  distinguished from full-text reading; incomplete historical attributions and limited reading scope remain explicit.
- Mermaid guidance now requires accessible titles/descriptions, explicit text colour for light-filled nodes and light/dark renders.
  The template permits a short onward-reading route; alt text does not substitute for legible embedded labels.
- Standalone SVG figures now use compact, stacked layouts and larger labels, with direct counts, matching line/legend patterns
  and light/dark palettes. Recorded values, accessible descriptions, original metadata and Markdown captions are retained. The two
  older path-text figures now also have selectable text, accessible titles/descriptions and explicit neuron-versus-head units.
- Circuit figures wrap long labels and stack independent branches for narrow screens. Count/comparison menus and the register-machine
  figure use larger type; wrapped panel headings have room above their nodes. Original computational edges, label values, trained
  membership, accessible descriptions and captions are retained. Invisible spacing links are layout constraints, not new computation.

### Corrected

- README's scratchpad diagram now separates the comparison boxes and keeps result labels clear of their arrows. Solid outlines
  identify every formula-set part as frozen; the design counts, worked values and stated limits are unchanged.
- [Comparison of counts](entries/compare-counts.md): the catalogue's held-out range now names fraction .8, its ten seeds and the
  copied .917–.963 range; the 30-network family census and universal held-out tie failure remain separate.
- README's [subtraction scrub](entries/subtraction.md#5-mechanism-and-verification) now includes the documented 416 fit and 416
  evaluation pairs, three donor draws and adaptive keep-set selection, replacing the earlier unavailable-count statement for this row.
  The failed scores remain failed; the glossary no longer implies that different runs use independent evaluation data.
- [Modular addition](entries/modular-addition-mlp.md): the earlier Partly solved task label conflated a clock-family observation with
  held-out success. The headline cell is Unsolved at its stated criterion; the separate formula status remains Partial.
- [Open-bracket counting](entries/open-bracket-count.md): the cosine-family census is not a held-out success rate; no seed reaches
  the stated held-out criterion. Its training-inclusive approximation is not a verified mechanism.
- [Chess policy](entries/chess-krk.md): reaching a threshold accuracy is distinct from exact policy matching; unfinished seeds remain
  unfinished. [Move selection](entries/chess-mate-in-one.md) now distinguishes completed screening from missing final replication.
- [Output-order conversion](entries/output-order-base-conversion.md): rounded protocol stop labels do not establish exact test success.
- [Finite-field systems](entries/linear-systems-finite-fields.md): reference-keyed readout substitution does not establish the trained
  network's internal operand computation; whole-circuit complement failures remain.
- [Polynomial multiplication](entries/polynomial-add-mul.md): frozen-product-gate screening improves accuracy without establishing
  exact multiplication or a verified whole-network algorithm. Local substitution counts refer to snapshots where stated.
- README's earlier generic all-input HAND guarantee now defers to each design's exactness scope and failures. Its circuit-size
  comparison names the designs and units rather than claiming one common order-of-magnitude reduction. Historical scrub scores
  and edge-overlap ranges remain labelled, with selected-seed, cohort and missing-example-count caveats.
- [Arithmetic](INDEX.md#arithmetic) summaries retain failed mechanism tests and distinguish limited local forms from whole-network
  explanations. [External-zoo](INDEX.md#external-model-zoos) summaries retain optimal-only, empty-complement and other flags;
  sampled certificates are not all-input theorems or task-accuracy guarantees.

## Maintenance policy

On publication, move reviewed changes to a dated release section and link the exact atlas and archive revisions. Record substantive
changes to findings as corrections, with the earlier claim, the reason and the affected entry. Do not erase superseded failures or
rewrite them as passes. Use a new archived version when released files change; a metadata-only correction is different.

The format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/) (community guidance). Archival versioning follows
[Zenodo's version guidance](https://help.zenodo.org/docs/deposit/manage-versions/) (repository documentation). This policy is not a
claim that a Zenodo record or tagged release already exists.
